cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  no_peak: a browser port of the CLUSTER pulse-detection
  algorithm
message: >-
  CLUSTER is not this project's algorithm. If you use this
  software, cite Veldhuis and Johnson 1986 — listed under
  `references` below, and the citation the field uses for the
  program itself — and cite this port as well only if the
  implementation, not the method, is what you are pointing at.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: DeFazio
    given-names: Richard
license: MIT
repository-code: 'https://github.com/syncytium2/no_peak'
url: 'https://nopeak.tonydefazio.com'
abstract: >-
  A client-side browser implementation of CLUSTER, the
  Veldhuis–Johnson moving-window pooled-t algorithm for
  detecting pulses in endocrine time series. Uploaded data
  never leaves the reader's machine: there is no server, no
  account and no analytics. The port follows two reference
  implementations — Michael Johnson's CLUST5 Fortran and the
  Igor Pro Cluster package — and can run as either, since the
  two disagree; neither reference implementation is
  redistributed here. Output is checked against the
  originals' own listings on data that is not public, so the
  parity suites are absent from a clone; docs/validation-status.md
  is the honest accounting of what has and has not been
  checked, and /methods is the standalone algorithm
  reference. A preliminary version, still under review.
keywords:
  - pulse detection
  - hormone pulsatility
  - endocrinology
  - time series
  - CLUSTER
  - research software
  - reproducibility
references:
  - type: article
    title: >-
      Cluster analysis: a simple, versatile, and robust
      algorithm for endocrine pulse detection
    authors:
      - family-names: Veldhuis
        given-names: Johannes D.
      - family-names: Johnson
        given-names: Michael L.
    journal: American Journal of Physiology
    year: 1986
    month: 4
    volume: 250
    issue: '4 Pt 1'
    start: E486
    end: E493
    doi: 10.1152/ajpendo.1986.250.4.E486
    notes: 'PMID 3008572'
# No `version:` or `date-released:` on purpose. There is no release, and a
# version here would be a second hand-maintained copy of package.json's — the
# two-lists-that-disagree failure this repo already fights in the vendor gate.
# Add both together, from the tag, if a release is ever cut.
